import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleOffer from '../../components/ArticleOffer.astro';

const faq = [
  { q: "What is io_method in PostgreSQL 18?", a: "It selects how PostgreSQL 18 performs asynchronous reads: worker hands them to background I/O worker processes, io_uring uses the Linux io_uring interface, and sync performs them synchronously as PostgreSQL 17 did." },
  { q: "What is the default io_method?", a: "worker, with io_workers set to 3. A cluster upgraded to PostgreSQL 18 uses asynchronous reads without any configuration change." },
  { q: "Does asynchronous I/O make every query faster?", a: "No. In PostgreSQL 18 it applies to reads by sequential scans, bitmap heap scans and vacuum. Index scans, writes and WAL are still synchronous. In the lab, worker cut a cold sequential scan by 34 to 40 percent but did not measurably change a bitmap heap scan." },
  { q: "Should I use io_uring instead of worker?", a: "Only after measuring. io_uring needs Linux, a PostgreSQL build with liburing, and a kernel and container runtime that allow it; Docker's default seccomp profile blocks it. In the lab it was about 22 percent faster than sync on a bitmap heap scan but slower than worker on a sequential scan." },
  { q: "Does changing io_method need a restart?", a: "Yes. io_method can only be set at server start. effective_io_concurrency can be changed per session." },
  { q: "What is the default effective_io_concurrency in PostgreSQL 18?", a: "16, up from 1 in earlier versions. In the lab, raising it to 64 or 256 made no consistent difference to a bitmap scan that read 98 percent of the table in physical order." },
  { q: "How can I tell whether a query uses asynchronous I/O?", a: "Query the pg_aios view while the statement runs; in-flight reads appear there. With io_method set to worker, pg_stat_activity also shows io worker processes. If pg_aios stays empty during your slow query, io_method is not the setting that will help it." },
  { q: "Can I change io_method on RDS or Aurora?", a: "Check whether your parameter group exposes it before planning around it. This guide's lab ran self-managed PostgreSQL 18.6 and did not test RDS or Aurora." }
];
---
<ArticleLayout title="PostgreSQL 18 Async I/O: Does io_method Make Queries Faster?" description="Tested on PostgreSQL 18.6: io_method=worker cut a cold sequential scan by a third, io_uring won on a bitmap heap scan, and neither helped everywhere. How to measure it on your own system." eyebrow="PostgreSQL performance · Lab-tested" published="2026-10-27" updated="2026-10-27" faq={faq} ctaTitle="Upgrading to PostgreSQL 18 and want to know what will actually get faster?" ctaDescription="A scoped investigation measures your top statements before and after the change, separates I/O wait from CPU, and tells you which settings are worth touching on your storage.">
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> sometimes, and only for reads. PostgreSQL 18 added an asynchronous I/O subsystem that sequential scans, bitmap heap scans and vacuum use to read ahead. The default, <code>io_method = worker</code>, is already on after an upgrade. In this lab it made a cold sequential scan about a third faster, did nothing measurable for a bitmap heap scan, and <code>io_uring</code> did the opposite. Which one helps depends on the query and the storage, so measure before you change it.</p>
  <p>The lab used PostgreSQL 18.6 in Docker on a laptop with a local SSD, with parallel query off and the page cache dropped before every run. The <a href="https://github.com/kiransabne04/databasedeveloper.dev/tree/main/labs/pg18-async-io">script and full output</a> are public.</p>

  <h2>What changed in PostgreSQL 18</h2>
  <p>Before 18, a backend asked the operating system for one 8 kB page at a time and waited for each read, with some prefetch hints for bitmap scans. From 18, eligible reads are issued ahead of time and the backend only waits if the data has not arrived when it needs it. Three settings control it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>io_method</code>: <code>worker</code> (default) hands reads to background I/O worker processes; <code>io_uring</code> uses the Linux kernel interface and needs a build with liburing; <code>sync</code> behaves like PostgreSQL 17. Changing it needs a restart.</li>
    <li><code>io_workers</code>: number of I/O workers when <code>io_method = worker</code>. Default 3.</li>
    <li><code>effective_io_concurrency</code>: how many reads a session keeps in flight. The default rose from 1 to 16 in 18.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Writes, index scans and WAL are still synchronous in 18. If your slow statements are index lookups or commits, async I/O will not change them.</p>

  <h2>Results: three methods, two query shapes</h2>
  <p>An <code>events</code> table of 8 million rows (1.2 GB) and a 256 MB <code>shared_buffers</code>, so every run read from storage. Five cold runs per setting, execution time in milliseconds. <code>sync</code> ran first and again last to check the baseline did not drift:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>io_method</th><th>Sequential scan, 5 runs</th><th>Median</th><th>Bitmap heap scan, 5 runs</th><th>Median</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>sync</code></td><td>402 404 378 375 465</td><td>402</td><td>348 345 346 514 459</td><td>348</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>worker</code></td><td>263 287 267 267 259</td><td><strong>267</strong></td><td>365 345 325 330 334</td><td>334</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>io_uring</code></td><td>329 330 328 398 327</td><td>329</td><td>271 259 268 292 259</td><td><strong>268</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sync</code> again</td><td>445 496 383 373 466</td><td>445</td><td>338 366 333 346 339</td><td>339</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>For the sequential scan, <code>worker</code> was 34–40% faster than <code>sync</code> and <code>io_uring</code> 18–26%. For the bitmap heap scan, <code>worker</code> was within noise of <code>sync</code> and <code>io_uring</code> was about 22% faster. The plans show where the time went: <code>I/O Timings</code> is time the backend spent waiting for reads.</p>
<pre><code>{`-- Sequential scan, 153,847 pages read
io_method=sync      I/O Timings: shared read=160.178   Execution Time: 392.861 ms
io_method=worker    I/O Timings: shared read=24.159    Execution Time: 254.596 ms
io_method=io_uring  I/O Timings: shared read=36.071    Execution Time: 328.108 ms

-- Bitmap heap scan, 151,644 pages read
io_method=sync      I/O Timings: shared read=184.557   Execution Time: 350.437 ms
io_method=worker    I/O Timings: shared read=218.860   Execution Time: 336.057 ms
io_method=io_uring  I/O Timings: shared read=86.969    Execution Time: 285.155 ms`}</code></pre>
  <p>With <code>worker</code>, the sequential scan waited for reads for 24 ms instead of 160 ms because the reads were already done when it reached each page. The bitmap scan's wait barely changed with <code>worker</code> but halved with <code>io_uring</code>. I did not investigate why the two methods split this way; the point is that they did, on the same data and the same machine.</p>
  <p>Raising <code>effective_io_concurrency</code> from 16 to 64 or 256 made no consistent difference to the bitmap scan here. That query read 98% of the table's pages in physical order, which is close to a sequential read. A bitmap scan that jumps across a large table on network storage is where prefetch depth should matter more.</p>

  <h2>Why your numbers will differ</h2>
  <p>This laptop is close to the worst case for async I/O. Docker Desktop runs Linux in a VM, and dropping the Linux page cache does not clear the macOS cache underneath, so "cold" reads still returned in microseconds. Async I/O helps most when each read takes long, which is the normal case on network-attached storage such as EBS. So the gain on a real server can be larger, smaller or zero, and the only number that matters is yours. On RDS and Aurora, check which of these parameters your parameter group exposes before planning around them; I have not tested them there.</p>

  <ArticleOffer offer="investigation" title="Moving from PostgreSQL 14 to 18 and expecting it to be faster?" text="An investigation baselines your top statements before the upgrade, measures them after it, and shows which got faster, which regressed and why, so you are not guessing from release notes." />

  <h2>How to see async I/O working</h2>
  <p>I/O workers are ordinary backend processes, and in-flight reads are visible in the new <code>pg_aios</code> view. Sampled during a cold sequential scan with <code>io_method = worker</code>:</p>
<pre><code>{`SELECT count(*), string_agg(DISTINCT operation || '/' || state, ', ') FROM pg_aios;
 11 | readv/COMPLETED_SHARED, readv/SUBMITTED

SELECT backend_type, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1;
 io worker                    |     3
 client backend               |     1
 ...`}</code></pre>
  <p>Around ten reads were in flight at any moment. If <code>pg_aios</code> stays empty while your slow query runs, that query is not using async reads and <code>io_method</code> is not your lever.</p>

  <h2>Should you change io_method?</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Leave the default first.</strong> <code>worker</code> works everywhere and gave the largest sequential-scan gain here.</li>
    <li><strong>Find out whether reads are your problem.</strong> Rank statements in <a href="/insights/postgresql-slow-queries-pg-stat-statements"><code>pg_stat_statements</code></a> by total time, then compare <code>shared_blk_read_time</code> with total execution time. If read wait is a small share, no I/O setting will help much.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the plan shape.</strong> Only sequential scans, bitmap heap scans and vacuum use async reads in 18. Look for them with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>, which in 18 shows buffers by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Test <code>io_uring</code> on a copy.</strong> It needs Linux, a liburing build and a kernel and container runtime that allow it. Docker's default seccomp profile blocks it; this lab used <code>--privileged</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Compare on the real storage at real concurrency.</strong> Same statements, same data, cold and warm, at each setting. Under load, 3 I/O workers may become the queue; raise <code>io_workers</code> and measure again.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>PostgreSQL 19 reached release candidate 1 on 15 October 2026. Check its release notes before tuning <code>io_workers</code> by hand on a new cluster. For the measurement method itself, see <a href="/insights/how-to-profile-slow-postgresql-queries">how to profile slow PostgreSQL queries</a>, and if you are upgrading from 14, the <a href="/postgresql-14-upgrade-checklist">PostgreSQL 14 upgrade checklist</a>.</p>

